# SYNOPSIS

```usage
bootfs
```

# DESCRIPTION

`bootfs` serves the boot image, `bootfs.tar`, as a read-only 9Px tree posted as `/srv/bootfs`.
It is the root of nearly every namespace: the POSIX template (`/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) mounts it on `/`, and most manifests (see svc(6)) start with `mount=/ srv=bootfs`.

svcd(8) starts it among the first services, from the manifest `boot/svc/bootfs.ndb`, and gives it the image, a read-only VMO the bootloader loaded as a module, and a listen channel; it restarts it if it exits.
`bootfs` maps the image and makes a node for each entry, and for each directory the archive only implies.
Nothing is copied: a read comes straight from the mapped archive.
It takes no arguments.

The image is a ustar archive that `./build` makes: regular files, directories, and hard links to a file earlier in the archive, served as that file, as sbase's programs are one program under many names.
Every header is checked, and every path must be relative, with no empty, `.` or `..` part.
Its entries keep the archive's order in a listing.

Every file is owned by `boot`, has no times, and has the archive's permissions with every write bit cleared; an implied directory is `0555`.
Anything opened for writing, truncating or removing on close is refused, and nothing can be made, removed or changed.

An aname attaches below the root: `boot/svc` serves only that directory, and `..` stops there.
An aname that names a file, or holds `..` or a part that does not exist, is refused.

# EXAMPLES

Its manifest, `boot/svc/bootfs.ndb`:

```ndb
service=bootfs program=/boot/bin/bootfs post=bootfs bootimage console restart
```

and what it prints:

```text
bootfs: serving 170 files in 33 directories
```

The namespace test mounts it twice, the second time its `boot/svc` alone, after `/dev`:

```ndb
mount=/ srv=bootfs
bind=/bin new=/boot/bin flags=a
mount=/dev srv=bootfs aname=boot/svc flags=a
```

# FILES

: `/srv/bootfs`
  Its post.
: `/boot/bin`
  The programs.
: `/boot/svc`, `/boot/drv`
  The service and driver manifests svcd(8) and devmgr(8) read.
: `/lib/ns`
  The namespace templates.
: `/adm`, `/bin`, `/dev`, `/dist`, `/n`, `/net`, `/proc`, `/srv`, `/sys`, `/tmp`
  Empty directories, mount points for the namespace.

# SEE ALSO

svcd(8), svc(6), namespace(6), tmpfs(4), nullfs(4)

# DIAGNOSTICS

At start it prints how many files and directories it serves.
A failure to start prints `bootfs: FAILED:` and what failed, and exits with it:

: `no boot image or listen channel in the spawn message`
  svcd gave it no image, no size for it, or no post.
: `cannot map the boot image`
  The image could not be mapped.
: `the boot image is malformed`
  A header, a field or a path in the archive is bad.
: `the boot image has too many entries`
  More than 1022 files and directories below the root, or more than 64 KiB of names.
: `the boot image has a file and a directory with one name`
  Two entries clash.

It exits `cannot serve` if serving fails.

# BUGS

The tree has room for 1022 files and directories below the root and 64 KiB of names, whatever the image holds.

Files have no times: every modification and access time is 0.

A file's offset is not shared with a child, as it is on a server with the posix extension: a POSIX child opens the file again.
